.Dd October 4, 2026
.Dt ARCH_PRCTL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm arch_prctl
.Nd set or get the thread's FS and GS bases on x86-64
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Fd "#define _GNU_SOURCE"
.In unistd.h
.In sys/syscall.h
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "long number" "..."
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the x86-64 kernel's call, number 158; the i686 kernel does not have it,
and a program there sets its thread pointer with
.Xr set_thread_area 2 .
Musl declares no function for it; use
.Fn syscall
with
.Dv SYS_arch_prctl ,
.Fa code
and
.Fa addr .
Musl's
.Fn __set_thread_area
issues the call itself, with
.Dv ARCH_SET_FS ,
to install the thread-local storage of the first thread.
.Pp
Each thread has two bases of its own, kept in its process record, saved
when it switches away and loaded when it switches back;
.Xr fork 2
gives the child its parent's as they are at the fork, and
.Xr execve 2
sets both to 0.
A selector the program loads into FS or GS sets that base to the
descriptor's, 0, as on Linux, and
.Dv ARCH_GET_FS
and
.Dv ARCH_GET_GS
then read 0.
FS's base is the thread pointer, where the thread's thread-local storage
begins; GS's base is the program's to use as it likes.
.Fa code
selects the operation:
.Bl -tag -width "ARCH_SET_FS"
.It Dv ARCH_SET_FS No (0x1002)
Make
.Fa addr
the thread's FS base.
.It Dv ARCH_GET_FS No (0x1003)
Store the thread's FS base, a 64-bit word, at the user address
.Fa addr .
.It Dv ARCH_SET_GS No (0x1001)
Make
.Fa addr
the thread's GS base.
.It Dv ARCH_GET_GS No (0x1004)
Store the thread's GS base at the user address
.Fa addr .
.El
.Pp
A base must be a user address, below 0x00007ffffffff000, the end of the
range a program may map.
A thread
.Xr clone 2
makes with
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS
gets the clone's TLS argument as its FS base, which must be such an
address too.
.Xr ptrace 2 Ns 's
.Dv PTRACE_ARCH_PRCTL
does the same operations on a stopped tracee, with the operation in
.Fa data
and the base or the address in
.Fa addr .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
0, or -1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EPERM
A base to set is not below 0x00007ffffffff000.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Dv ARCH_GET_FS
or
.Dv ARCH_GET_GS
was given an address that cannot be written.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa code
is none of the four.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clone 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr ptrace 2 ,
.Xr set_thread_area 2
.Sh STANDARDS
Linux's x86-64 call, with the same number and codes.
Linux also takes
.Dv ARCH_GET_CPUID ,
.Dv ARCH_SET_CPUID ,
the vDSO's and the extended state's codes and those of the shadow stack;
this kernel refuses them with
.Er EINVAL .
